error: flag provided but not defined: -real.json
Usage: spm validate [options] [files...]

Validate one or more security profiles.
Reads from stdin when no files are provided: a single profile, or a JSON
array of profiles. A file argument of - reads stdin beside the files.
Flags must precede file arguments, and -- ends them.
Exit status: 0 when every profile is valid, 1 when one is invalid, of
another type, or cannot be read, or the output cannot be written, 2 on a
usage error; 1 when a usage error comes up after a profile was found
invalid.
Writes the validated profiles on success, in the shape they came in: a
single profile as an object, and several, or a JSON array on stdin even of
one element, as an array. --quiet writes no profile and notes no
auto-detected profile type, and --no-detect-note drops that note while
still writing the profiles. Errors and warnings always go to stderr.
--quiet cannot be combined with --output or --format, nor --strict with
--artifact.

--arch names the native seccomp architecture of the node that loads the
profiles, which decides what --artifact and --strict accept: a value above
32 bits is refused where that node covers a 32-bit architecture. It
defaults to the architecture this program runs on, so the same profile can
pass here and fail on another machine; pass a name such as SCMP_ARCH_X86_64
for the target node, or --arch none for a node of unknown architecture,
which refuses such a value whatever the profile lists. The default checks do
not depend on it, so it needs --artifact or --strict.

Options:
  --arch string
    	seccomp architecture of the node that loads the profiles, for --artifact or --strict: native, none, or a name such as SCMP_ARCH_X86_64 (default "native")
  --artifact
    	validate as an artifact, the untrusted profile a container runtime pulls; besides the repeated keys every mode rejects, it rejects unknown and misspelled fields, missing required members and invalid UTF-8
  --format string
    	output format: json, human (default "json")
  --no-detect-note
    	do not note an auto-detected profile type on stderr; the validated profiles, errors and warnings still go to their usual streams
  --output string
    	write output to file (default: stdout, not with --quiet)
  --quiet
    	write no profile on success and note no auto-detected type; errors and warnings still go to stderr (not with --output or --format)
  --strict
    	use strict validation, which also rejects unknown and misspelled fields, missing required members and invalid UTF-8 (not with --artifact)
  --type string
    	profile type: seccomp, apparmor, landlock (auto-detected if omitted)
